WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.400
- We wish that this had come
out a little more clearly

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.640
for everyone because we feel very positive

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.130
and strongly about what we're doing,

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:09.303
and we can see that it's
been a widely misunderstood.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.280
- [Joanna] It's not every
day an Apple executive

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.190
admits to widespread
confusion over new policies

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:17.890
related to fighting child pornography.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:19.970
Yet, here we are.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:21.970
- I grant you, in hindsight,

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.730
introducing these two
features at the same time

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:26.890
was a recipe for this kind of confusion.

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:28.140
- [Joanna] Last week, Apple introduced

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.030
new child protection features,

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.270
one related to spotting and reporting

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:34.920
illegal child sexual abuse material,

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.250
AKA CSAM, or child pornography.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:41.240
And another related to
spotting nudes in text messages

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.820
sent to and often from children.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.320
- It's really clear a lot of messages

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.080
got jumbled pretty badly.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.100
- [Joanna] A lot of people
got mad and got mad real fast.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.060
Not because they don't think
child pornography is horrible,

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.340
but because of the
seemingly controversial way

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.370
Apple was spotting this content

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.540
and a feeling that the
company was crossing the line

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.110
by scanning the contents of their phones.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:06.120
- I do believe the
soundbite that got out early

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:11.120
was, oh my God, Apple is
scanning my phone for images.

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:12.810
This is not what is happening.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.660
- I sat down with Apple software chief,

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.040
Craig Federighi, to ask some questions

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.460
that have been on my mind
about these features.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:21.350
- Good morning.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:22.410
- Good to see you.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.570
I broke the interview into two parts,

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.270
one, the features to spot CSAM.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.350
Two, the messaging feature.

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:32.030
It's all confusing, but
it's important to understand

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:35.433
what's happening and what
control you do and don't have.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:39.510
- In the simplest terms possible,

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:43.193
how is Apple looking for
child pornography on iPhones?

00:01:43.193 --> 00:01:44.890
- So to be clear, we're
not actually looking

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:47.140
for child pornography on iPhones.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.890
That's the first root
of the misunderstanding.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:54.220
What we're doing is we're
finding illegal images

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.690
of child pornography stored in iCloud.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:00.210
If you look at any other cloud service,

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.470
they currently are scanning photos

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:05.940
by looking at every
single photo in the cloud

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.610
and analyzing it.

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:10.610
We wanted to be able to spot
such photos in the cloud

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:13.220
without looking at people's photos

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:15.710
and came up with an
architecture to do this,

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:17.410
and it's very important we felt

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:20.180
before we could offer
this kind of capability

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.000
to do it in a way much, much more private

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.430
than anything that's been
done in this area before.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:28.090
- Okay, so let's break this
down a little bit more simply.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:29.860
So this is my iPhone.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:31.530
I get this update.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:34.380
What is happening to my
iCloud Photo Library?

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:37.480
- So if you are a customer that is using

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:39.350
iCloud Photo Library,
which you don't have to,

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:40.710
but if you're using iCloud Photo Library

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:43.190
to store your photos in the cloud,

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:45.443
then what's happening is a,

00:02:46.463 --> 00:02:49.810
a multi-part algorithm

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:53.450
where there's a degree of
analysis done on your device

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:56.310
as it uploads a photo to the cloud

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:57.700
so that the cloud then can do

00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:59.640
the other half of the algorithm.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.570
And if, and only if you meet a threshold

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:07.340
of something on the order of 30 known

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.500
child pornographic images matching,

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.840
only then does Apple know
anything about your account

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.380
and know anything about those images.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:19.140
And at that point, only
knows about those images,

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.080
not about any of your other images.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:24.410
And it should be clear this
isn't doing some analysis for,

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:28.400
did you have a picture of
your child in the bathtub

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.550
or for that matter, did you have a picture

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:32.640
of some pornography of any other sort?

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.990
This is literally only matching
on the exact fingerprints

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:40.910
of specific known child
pornographic images.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:41.920
- [Joanna] Let's pause and talk about

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:43.710
what's been so controversial here.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:46.890
The National Center for
Missing and Exploited Children,

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:50.290
or NCMEC has a database of known CSAM.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:52.460
Sorry for all the acronyms.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.453
Other big tech companies,
Google, Facebook, Microsoft

00:03:55.453 --> 00:03:57.610
scan photos you upload to the cloud

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:00.700
to find images that match
any in the repository

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:02.270
of NCMEC photos.

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:04.360
Instead, Apple decided
it wanted to do this

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:07.150
on your device for privacy reasons,

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:10.200
and that's what a lot of
this controversy is about.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.630
- On your phone, when images are uploaded

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:15.020
to the iCloud Photo Library,

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:18.360
as part of that upload pipeline,

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:20.930
there is a what's called a neural hash

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:22.260
performed on the image,

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:24.950
and that neural hash is then intersected

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:28.500
against a database that's on your device.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:29.850
- [Joanna] Okay, pause again.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:33.260
Illegal photos have been converted
into cryptographic codes,

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:36.190
or a string of numbers that
identify the characteristics

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:39.741
of an image, what they call neural hashes.

00:04:39.741 --> 00:04:42.978
When your own photo is about
to be uploaded to iCloud,

00:04:42.978 --> 00:04:44.800
the software generates a hash of it.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.260
The device cross-references
your image hash

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:49.170
to the CSAM hashes.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:50.260
- And what comes out of that

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:53.035
is basically some gobbledygook
that at that point,

00:04:53.035 --> 00:04:57.040
your phone doesn't know
whether it matched anything

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.470
and neither does Apple.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:02.050
But that thing is wrapped up
in what we call safety voucher,

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:04.940
and when the photo is stored,
so is the safety voucher.

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:06.030
- [Joanna] Pause again.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:08.880
The system makes a thing
called a safety voucher,

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.840
which holds the match result.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.280
That is uploaded with the photo to iCloud.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:15.710
- Then the second half of the process

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:19.540
occurs in the cloud where
these safety vouchers,

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:21.030
some math is done on the collection

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:22.460
of all the safety vouchers

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:27.110
and only if a threshold
of matches are achieved

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:30.700
after a second pass of math,
does anything get learned.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:31.980
- [Joanna] One last pause.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:35.050
In the cloud, if an account
collects around 30 vouchers

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:37.280
that correspond to illegal images,

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:40.330
the account will be flagged
to Apple's human moderators,

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:42.100
who then review the vouchers

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:45.390
and see if they actually
contain illegal images.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:47.530
If they do, Apple reports the account

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:49.330
to the proper authorities.

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:52.760
There's a sense of when I
upload something to the cloud,

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:55.950
I sort of think, okay, I've
got to trust this company,

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.640
but on my device, I've
been really led to believe,

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:01.000
in a large part because of
Apple's marketing efforts,

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.650
that this is mine.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:05.590
The info that's on here
is mine, it is private.

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:07.480
I think what's happening here is

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:09.110
you're coming onto the device.

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:11.260
You're making me feel a little bit like,

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:14.590
well, Apple can do things on your device.

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:16.590
- I think that's a common

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:19.010
but really profound misunderstanding.

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:22.290
This is only being applied
as part of the process

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:24.110
of storing something in the cloud.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:27.350
This isn't some processing
that's running over

00:06:27.350 --> 00:06:30.240
the images you store, you
know, in your messages

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.650
or in Telegram or anything else,

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:34.520
you know, what you're browsing on the web.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:36.480
And this literally is part of the pipeline

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:38.880
for storing images in iCloud.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.780
- Why did you decide to
release this right now?

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:42.590
Was there pressure to do it?

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:44.100
- No.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:48.120
Really it came down to, we figured it out.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:49.600
We've wanted to do something,

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:52.530
but we've been unwilling
to deploy a solution

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:56.010
that would involve
scanning all customer data.

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:57.357
- Tim Cook said earlier this year,

00:06:57.357 --> 00:06:59.320
"We've spoken out time and again-"

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:01.940
- For strong encryption without backdoors,

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:05.970
recognizing that security is
the foundation of privacy.

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.300
- But isn't this, in a way, a backdoor?

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:10.690
- Think in no way is this a backdoor.

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:11.943
I don't understand.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:16.100
I really don't understand
that characterization.

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:19.140
Imagine someone was scanning
images in the cloud.

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:22.200
Well, who knows what's being scanned for?

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:24.680
In our case, the database
is shipped on device.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:27.350
People can see, and it's a single image

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:28.610
across all countries.

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:30.770
We ship the same software in China

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:33.950
with the same database
as we ship in America,

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:35.170
as we ship in Europe.

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:37.900
If someone were to come to
Apple, Apple would say no,

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:39.210
but let's say you aren't confident.

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:42.040
You don't want to just
rely on Apple saying no.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:44.790
You want to be sure that Apple
couldn't get away with it

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:45.910
if we said yes.

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:48.290
Well that was the bar we set for ourselves

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:49.920
in releasing this kind of system.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:51.820
There are multiple levels of auditability,

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:54.590
and so we're making sure
that you don't have to trust

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:58.780
any one entity or even any one country,

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:02.640
as far as how these images are,

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:04.950
what images are part of this process.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:07.440
- I want to just move on
to the iMessage feature

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:08.273
which you guys are calling

00:08:08.273 --> 00:08:10.020
Communication Safety in Messages.

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:12.470
Simplest way possible, explain it to me,

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:15.260
no acronyms, no jargon.

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:16.418
How does this work?

00:08:16.418 --> 00:08:17.530
(laughs)

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:18.808
- I'll do my best.

00:08:18.808 --> 00:08:23.400
The capability is you turn
this feature on for your child

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:25.640
and your child received an image coming in

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:30.640
that was potentially, you
know, pornographic content.

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:33.080
The image would be blurred out.

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:36.130
The child would be told
this content may be unsafe.

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:37.830
And so the child can just ignore it.

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:40.400
That detection happened on device.

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:44.020
No one was told, it's
just local processing.

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:46.890
If the child then says,
oh, I want to look at it,

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:49.390
then it warns the child,
it tells them about

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:51.840
that this could be unsafe.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:53.290
These things can be harmful.

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:54.810
Are you sure you want to do this?

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:56.030
Child can say yes.

00:08:56.030 --> 00:08:58.230
Now if the child is 12 or under,

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:00.750
and the parent has turned on
this notification capability,

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:02.350
the child will then be told

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:04.610
if you choose to continue and view this,

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:06.870
your parent will be notified
that this has happened

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:08.980
because I think a legitimate fear

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:11.110
of some parents with young children is

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:13.070
what's happening that my
child is being coached

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:16.220
to keep from me while
they're being groomed?

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:18.830
And really we think
parents more than anyone

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:21.380
are in the best position to parent.

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:22.800
- Okay, so I just want to clarify.

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:26.080
This technology is different
than the technology

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:28.730
you're using with the CSAM?

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:30.940
- That's right, it is 100% different.

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:31.820
- Okay, I lied.

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:32.870
Another pause.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:35.470
This feature is using
on-device machine learning

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:38.240
to look for photos that
might contain nudity.

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:40.630
How confident are you in this,

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:42.720
the algorithms that are
looking at these photos?

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.600
I mean, how confident are
you guys gonna to be that

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:49.081
this is a nude image
and not a rocket ship?

00:09:49.081 --> 00:09:50.670
- Right?

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:53.520
So this is a machine learned classifier.

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:55.100
It's very good.

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:57.240
It's very accurate.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:00.040
It can make a mistake, right?

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:03.040
It could say a photo that-

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:05.340
It's unlikely to be a rocket ship,

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:08.740
but, you know, we've had a tough time

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:11.345
coming up with images
that we can fool it with

00:10:11.345 --> 00:10:12.560
to do our testing with,

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:14.440
'cause we wanted to be
able to go through flows

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:16.660
in the feature without
all looking at nudity,

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:18.660
and it's very hard to fool it,

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:20.210
but it can be fooled.

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:21.610
- Privacy experts spoke out

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:23.810
about both of these
announcements this week.

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:25.090
With the CSAM feature,

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:27.350
the Electronic Frontier
Foundation said Apple

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:29.550
was overreaching and creating a backdoor

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:31.940
and a technology that could be abused.

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:35.160
Many have signed a letter
against these features.

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:36.960
Who owns this phone?

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:38.250
There's this famous Apple ad,

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.783
the 1984 ad where Apple is
literally bashing Big Brother.

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:45.680
Yet with these updates,
there's this thought

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:47.830
that Apple can reach into our pocket

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:50.660
and change our phone in significant ways.

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:53.340
- I think our customers own their phones.

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:54.370
For sure.

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:56.360
I think the feature
we're talking about here,

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:59.610
one needs to just continue to be reminded

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:02.500
is about only applying to data customers

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:04.790
choose to store on Apple servers.

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:07.790
I do believe the soundbite
that got out early

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:12.790
was, oh my God, apple is
scanning my phone for images.

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:15.520
This is not what is happening.

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:18.830
This is about images that
are being stored in the cloud

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:21.270
in an architecture for identifying

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:25.700
in the most privacy-protecting
way we can imagine

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:26.890
performing that process

00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:30.410
and in the most auditable
and verifiable way possible.

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:31.530
- [Joanna] When this update arrives,

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:33.160
it could provide powerful tools

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:35.290
for preventing child endangerment,

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:37.250
but it's also a reminder of the control

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:40.200
that Apple and executives
like Mr. Federighi

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:42.477
have over the products that we own.

